---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import JwtDecoder from '@/components/tools/dev/JwtDecoder.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="jwt-decoder">
  <JwtDecoder client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to decode a JWT</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste the token. The tool removes a <code>Bearer </code> prefix automatically.</li>
      <li>The JWT decoder decodes the header and payload from Base64url and shows them as formatted JSON.</li>
      <li>The claims table explains each registered claim and converts <code>exp</code>, <code>iat</code> and <code>nbf</code> into local times, with the distance from now.</li>
      <li>If the token uses HS256, HS384 or HS512, paste the shared secret and press <strong>Verify</strong> to check the signature with WebCrypto.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why decoding a token here is safe</h2>
    <p>
      A server-rendered JWT debugger receives the token on its infrastructure (TLS terminators, load
      balancer access logs, error trackers and other systems in the request path), even if it promises not
      to log it. To paste a production access token into one is a credential disclosure.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page is a static file, and its JavaScript has no API and no fetch call. Your browser parses the
      token and verifies the signature with its WebCrypto implementation, and both are gone when you close
      the tab. To confirm this, disconnect from the network: the page still works.
    </p>

    <h2>The three parts of a JWT</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Part</th><th>Contents</th><th>Protected?</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Header</td><td>The signing algorithm (<code>alg</code>), type (<code>typ</code>) and often a key id (<code>kid</code>)</td><td>Covered by the signature, but readable by anyone</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payload</td><td>The claims: who the token is about, who issued it, when it expires</td><td>Covered by the signature, but readable by anyone</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Signature</td><td>An HMAC or a digital signature over <code>header.payload</code></td><td>The only part that proves anything</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The three parts are joined with dots and encoded with Base64url, which is not encryption. Never put
      data in a JWT payload that the holder of the token must not read.
    </p>

    <h2>Registered claims worth knowing</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Claim</th><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>iss</code></td><td>Issuer</td><td>Who minted the token. Verifiers should check this matches an expected value.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sub</code></td><td>Subject</td><td>The principal, usually a stable user identifier.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aud</code></td><td>Audience</td><td>Who the token is for. A service must reject tokens issued for a different audience.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>exp</code></td><td>Expiration</td><td>Seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC. Reject at or after this instant.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nbf</code></td><td>Not before</td><td>Reject before this instant.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>iat</code></td><td>Issued at</td><td>When it was created. Use it to detect a stale session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>jti</code></td><td>JWT ID</td><td>A unique id, so a token can be blacklisted or replay-detected.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Signature algorithms</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Family</th><th>Examples</th><th>Key</th><th>Verifiable here</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>HMAC</td><td>HS256, HS384, HS512</td><td>One shared secret, held by both issuer and verifier</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RSA</td><td>RS256, PS256</td><td>Private key signs, public key verifies</td><td>No. Needs the issuer public key</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ECDSA</td><td>ES256, ES384</td><td>Private key signs, public key verifies</td><td>No. Needs the issuer public key</td></tr>
        <tr><td>None</td><td><code>alg: none</code></td><td>No signature at all</td><td>Flagged as unsigned</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A verifier that trusts the <code>alg</code> field from the token itself can be tricked into accepting
      <code>alg: none</code>, or into verifying an RS256 token as HS256 using the public key as the HMAC
      secret. Always set the expected algorithm in your verification code. Do not read it from the token.
    </p>

    <h2>Debugging an "expired" token</h2>
    <p>
      When a token looks valid but the server rejects it, check: is your device clock correct? Does the
      <code>aud</code> claim match the service you call? Is the <code>iss</code> an issuer that service
      trusts? Is the <code>kid</code> a key the service still has? Was the token revoked on the server? A
      JWT cannot be un-issued, so revocation is always outside the token. The claims table on this page
      answers the first four questions.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
